The classification between qualified professionals and other persons was also held to be reasonable (and not arbitrary) since the nature of services provided by a qualified professional is bound to be of higher quality. The Courts also observed that the apprehension of professionals that they would be required to pay service tax even when their clients do not pay them was misplaced in view of section 68 providing requisite protection. Constitutional validity of the service tax on the consulting engineers, architects and the chartered accountants under article 14 of Constitution of India was also upheld in Chartered Accountants Association v. Union of India 2005 (179) ELT 129/[2007] 7 STT 29 (Guj.). It was held that levy of the service tax on the consulting engineers, architect and chartered accountants is within the legislative competence of the Parliament and that tax on services rendered by professionals is distinct from tax on profession. The Parliament is competent to enact the law under article 248 read with Entry 97 in Union List to the Constitution of India and, therefore, section 88 of the Finance Act, 1997 and section 116 of the Finance Act, 1998 are constitutionally valid.

It was observed that the classification between qualified professionals like the chartered accountants, architects and engineers, on one hand, and non-qualified persons rendering service in the similar field, on the other hand, is reasonable as the services rendered by qualified professionals are qualitatively higher. There being no other entry in the State List or Concurrent List which could possibly be referred to for the purpose of service tax, the legislative competence for enacting this law must be traced to article 248 read with Entry 97 in the Union List.

The High Court explained the distinction between a tax on profession and a tax on the service provided by a professional. The Court observed as follows—

"We do not agree (with the Counsel for the petitioners) because we are completely convinced that the Apex Court has been looking at the professional tax as the tax for having a privilege to carry on that profession in that State. In both the earlier referred cases of Western India Theatre and Kamtaprasad, the Supreme Court has very clearly said that the professional tax has no connection or link with the income thereof. It virtually means that a privilege to practise a profession has got no relation or link with the income thereof, which comes only as a result of the services provided by the professionals. Therefore, there is a clear distinction between the privilege to carry on profession and the services offered as professional. Which result into the income from that profession. Therefore in our opinion, the argument that the service tax could have been legitimately charged by the State Government under a State enactment is clearly incorrect and it has to be held that while the profession tax deals with the privilege to practise a particular profession, the present service tax deals with the "services" provided by the professionals and therefore, the two are distinct and separate and the service tax cannot be viewed or confused as a tax on profession.

On the contention that the tax was discriminatory in the sense that it was applied to Chartered Accountants and not to similar service provider, like advocates, the High Court observed, "This argument is obviously incorrect because it is for the Legislature to select any particular service or any particular subject for taxing. The celebrated judgment in Federation of Hotels and Restaurants case, cited supra, aptly covers the subject. In paragraph 20 the Apex Court observes that though the taxing laws are outside Article 14, however, having regard to the wide variety of diverse economic criteria that go into the formulation of a fiscal policy Legislature enjoys a wide latitude in the matter of selection of persons, subject-matter, events, etc. for taxation. The tests of the vice of discrimination in a taxing law are, accordingly, less rigorous. In examining the allegations of a hostile, discriminatory treatment what is looked into is not its phraseology, but the real effect of its provisions."

Taxable service

4. Section 65(105 )(s) defines ‘taxable service’ as under—

"‘taxable service’ means the service provided or to be provided.—

to a client, by a Practising Chartered Accountant in his professional capacity, in any manner."

Thus, taxable service would include rendering of professional services in the professional capacity in the field of auditing and accountancy. The chartered accountants, practising in their individual names and also those practising as partners of firms shall be liable for service tax in respect of only notified services provided as practising accountants. In case of firm of chartered accountants, firm is the service provider, and not its partners in their individual capacity. Services provided by the chartered accountants normally include—

(i)Accounting services;

(ii)Auditing;

(iii)Taxation;

(iv)Corporate matters/consultancy;

(v)Management consultancy;

(vi)Project finance and consultancy;

(vii)Other consultancy;

(viii)Certification work;

(ix)Any other service in professional capacity.

The assessee took Cenvat credit in respect of consultancy and professional fees paid to Chartered Accountants.

The Department denied credit on ground that -

Chartered Accountant who had raised the bills on the assessee had mentioned that it was for legal services in respect of sales tax matters, banking facilities and other legal consultation in various matters (without identifying the nature of services) ;

such services came in to service tax net with effect from 01.03.2011 ;

while impugned services were rendered during the period 2006-07 to 2009-10.

Assessee filed appeal before Tribunal.

 the balance sheet’. The main objects of any audit are : (a) to certify to the correctness of the financial position as to shown in the balance-sheet, and the accompanying revenue statements; (b) the detection of errors; (c) the detection of fraud - the detection of fraud is generally regarded as being of primary importance." Thus, the word ‘audit’ has a specific connotation in the matter of examination, investigation and auditing of accounts, where detection of fraud is of primary importance. 4. The challenge in a nutshell is to the exclusion of advocates and sales tax practitioners from doing the work of audit under section 61. To consider the challenge under articles 14 and 19(1)(g) one may refer to section 44AB of the Income-tax Act, 1961. This provision as in the instant case provides for audit of persons whose total sales or turnover or gross receipts exceed forty lakhs per year. This provision was challenged before the various High Courts. The Supreme Court in an appeal before it, has upheld the legality of the section. Once the Supreme Court in T.D. Venkata Rao v. Union of India [1999] 237 ITR 315/103 Taxman 621 has upheld the legality of section 44AB where the same terminology was used and which was also a provision pertaining to audit, and considering the object of both the provisions which is prevention of evasion of tax dues, the challenge by the petitioners on the ground of infraction of articles 14 and 19 of the Constitution will have to be similarly rejected. There are practically no distinguishing features. The only distinction, if any, is that, whereas section 44AB of 1961 Act is for the purpose of ascertaining ‘total income’, section 61 is for certification whether VAT has been correctly assessed, collected and paid.

5. The challenge based on article 14 is rejected by High Court on the following grounds:

(i) Chartered accountants by reason of their training have special aptitude in the matter of audit. An income-tax practitioner does not have the same expertise as the chartered accountants in the matter of accounts. The argument, therefore, that the effect of such a provision will be to exclude all other categories of authorized representatives except the chartered accountants from carrying on their profession is liable to be rejected, as they constitute two distinct classes having a nexus with the object of the provisions, which is evasion of tax dues;

(ii) The contention that such a provision brings in an oppressive restriction is also liable to be rejected as auditing accounts is a specialized job. It may be true that some income-tax practitioners may also acquire that skill by sheer practice without passing the necessary examination; but that does not preclude Parliament from prescribing special qualification with reference to the auditing of accounts;

(iii) Legal practitioners and chartered accountants are equal for the purpose of representation of assessee before assessing authority but they are not equal for the purpose of compulsory audit. The preferential treatment given to the chartered accountants for the purpose of compulsory audit does not militate against the rule of equality under article 14 of the Constitution. The terms "audit", "auditing" and the "functions of auditor" clearly bring about the difference between the chartered accountants and others. The object and purpose in providing compulsory audit is to facilitate the prevention of evasion of taxes; administrative convenience in quick and proper completion of assessments, etc. In the light of this object, chartered accountants and others cannot be said to be similarly situate. The qualifications and eligibility to be enrolled as income-tax practitioners are entirely different from that of chartered accountants from the point of view of auditing;

(iv) Merely because apart from dealers whose turnover is more than 40 lakhs, dealers dealing in liquor trade have also to get their accounts audited does not make the provision arbitrary. Such dealers are a class by themselves as they are carrying on a trade which is res extra commercium. They constitute a class by themselves and if the Legislature in its wisdom has provided that their accounts should be audited, it is neither unreasonable nor treating them as a class is arbitrary.

Thus, the High Court upheld the classification as reasonable and held that the classification has a nexus with the object which is to direct a class of dealers to get their accounts audited by a specialized agency so that there is no tax evasion. According to High Court the settled-law on the subject is that as long as the twin tests of reasonableness of the classification and nexus with the object are satisfied, wisdom of the legislation cannot be substituted. As regards the challenge under article 19(1)(g), the High Court held that it is clear that in the instant case considering section 82, the category of persons who are excluded from the ambit of the Explanation to section 61 are not denied their right of appearance before the authorities under the Act. In other words, they are not prohibited from carrying on their profession. In the matter of carrying out audit the State has chosen to confer that right only on a class of persons having expertise in the field. This cannot be said to be arbitrary or excessive in nature so as to go beyond the requirement of the interest of the general public. That would be yet another reason as to why the challenge under article 19(1)(g) must fail.

