These formats illustrate how to structure certificates so that they are consistent with the Guidance Note, including clear titles, specific addressees, identification of subject matter and applicable criteria, sections on management's responsibility and practitioner's responsibility, reference to the Guidance Note and SQC 1, clear form of conclusion (positive or negative), basis paragraphs, restriction of use where appropriate, and proper signature and UDIN placeholders. The Handbook clarifies that these formats are illustrative and that members should adapt them to their specific facts and legal requirements while preserving key assurance elements.

10. From ad hoc certificates to disciplined assurance engagements

The cumulative message of the Guidance Note and the Handbook is that certificates issued by Chartered Accountants must be treated as structured assurance engagements, not as informal letters. Firms are encouraged to institutionalise the checklist utility, maintain an Index register, consciously determine whether engagements provide reasonable or limited assurance, appropriately express unmodified or modified conclusions and integrate UDIN into their documentation.

By doing so, CAs can ensure that even a one page certificate stands on a robust foundation of engagement acceptance, planning, evidence and clear reporting. In an environment where certificates are increasingly revisited in regulatory proceedings, bank disputes and peer reviews, this shift from casual compliance to disciplined assurance is not optional; it is essential to protecting both the public interest and the practitioner.

