'Total sales', 'turnover' and 'gross receipts', defined

6G.2 The three expressions used by the legislation, viz., the 'total sales', 'turnover' or 'gross receipts' though not defined under the 1961 Act, in the ordinary sense, refer to the volume of the business to which it relates and which is/are carried on by the assessee and in making assessment of profits and gains from the business whether such volume is a part of the business concerns trading in commodities or otherwise the business activities where the assessee has to indulge in incurring cost before receiving the amount in relation to that business or he is carrying on other business activities in which the cost factor is excluded by the assessee and what he is receiving as charges for the work done by him, like job work, where the raw material is provided by the other manufacturer, the assessee is merely to relate his receipts to labour charges or procuring cost incurred by him along with part of his profit. It is in that sense that business which is carried on by the assessee has to be taken in totality. The 'sales', 'turnover' and 'gross receipts' are not words of art used in relation to any individual transaction independently but have been used as 'sales', 'turnover' or 'gross receipts'. The expression 'total' qualifies all the other three expressions, viz., 'sales', 'turnover' and 'gross receipts'. Total sales indicate the aggregate price of the sales of commodities carried out by the assessee as a trading business—Bajrang Oil Mills v. ITO [2007] 163 Taxman 154 (Raj.).

Where assessee claimed that transportation receipts were not to be included in total turnover and after excluding same his turnover was less than Rs. 40 lakhs, but did not file any documents to support claim that such receipts were to be excluded, these transportation receipts were to be included in 'total turnover' for purpose of applying section 44AB—Sardari Lal Oberai v. ITO [2007] 106 TTJ (Chd. - Trib.) 1033.

6G.2-1 'Turnover', inclusions and exclusions [ICAI's Guidance Note on Tax Audit] - It is to be noted that the method of accounting followed by the assessee is relevant for the determination of sales, turnover or gross receipts. The term "turnover" may be interpreted to mean the aggregate amount for which sales are effected or services rendered by an enterprise.

Therefore, the definition of "turnover" shall include the following, or those amounts which shall not be deducted from the "turnover":—

(a)

If excise duty and/or sales tax are included in the sales price;

(b)

Sales of scrap shown separately under the head 'Miscellaneous income';
(c)

Commission allowed to third parties;

(d)

Cash discount otherwise than that allowed in a cash memo or sales invoice since it is in the nature of a financing charge;

(e)

Special rebate if it is in the nature of commission on sales;

(f)

Sale proceeds of property (including any shares, securities, etc.) held as stock-in-trade.

Where assessee was appointed as a distributor on principal-to-principal basis for sale of gas cylinders to consumers, sale of gas cylinders was to be included in the 'turnover' of such assessee-Attara Gas Service v. CIT [2014] 50 taxmann.com 445/227 Taxman 159 (Mag.) (All.).

Similarly, the definition of "turnover" shall exclude the following, or deducted from the "turnover": —

(a)

Trade discounts allowed in the sales invoice;

(b)

If excise duty or sales tax recovered are credited separately to their relevant accounts and payments to the authority are debited in the said accounts;

(c)

Turnover discount (which is in the nature of trade discount) even if the same is allowed at periodical intervals by separate credit notes;

(d)

Special rebate if it is in the nature of trade discount;

(e)

Sales return even if such returns are from the sales made in earlier years;

