The classification between qualified professionals and other persons was also held to be reasonable (and not arbitrary) since the nature of services provided by a qualified professional is bound to be of higher quality. The Courts also observed that the apprehension of professionals that they would be required to pay service tax even when their clients do not pay them was misplaced in view of section 68 providing requisite protection. Constitutional validity of the service tax on the consulting engineers, architects and the chartered accountants under article 14 of Constitution of India was also upheld in Chartered Accountants Association v. Union of India 2005 (179) ELT 129/[2007] 7 STT 29 (Guj.). It was held that levy of the service tax on the consulting engineers, architect and chartered accountants is within the legislative competence of the Parliament and that tax on services rendered by professionals is distinct from tax on profession. The Parliament is competent to enact the law under article 248 read with Entry 97 in Union List to the Constitution of India and, therefore, section 88 of the Finance Act, 1997 and section 116 of the Finance Act, 1998 are constitutionally valid.

It was observed that the classification between qualified professionals like the chartered accountants, architects and engineers, on one hand, and non-qualified persons rendering service in the similar field, on the other hand, is reasonable as the services rendered by qualified professionals are qualitatively higher. There being no other entry in the State List or Concurrent List which could possibly be referred to for the purpose of service tax, the legislative competence for enacting this law must be traced to article 248 read with Entry 97 in the Union List.

The High Court explained the distinction between a tax on profession and a tax on the service provided by a professional. The Court observed as follows—

"We do not agree (with the Counsel for the petitioners) because we are completely convinced that the Apex Court has been looking at the professional tax as the tax for having a privilege to carry on that profession in that State. In both the earlier referred cases of Western India Theatre and Kamtaprasad, the Supreme Court has very clearly said that the professional tax has no connection or link with the income thereof. It virtually means that a privilege to practise a profession has got no relation or link with the income thereof, which comes only as a result of the services provided by the professionals. Therefore, there is a clear distinction between the privilege to carry on profession and the services offered as professional. Which result into the income from that profession. Therefore in our opinion, the argument that the service tax could have been legitimately charged by the State Government under a State enactment is clearly incorrect and it has to be held that while the profession tax deals with the privilege to practise a particular profession, the present service tax deals with the "services" provided by the professionals and therefore, the two are distinct and separate and the service tax cannot be viewed or confused as a tax on profession.

On the contention that the tax was discriminatory in the sense that it was applied to Chartered Accountants and not to similar service provider, like advocates, the High Court observed, "This argument is obviously incorrect because it is for the Legislature to select any particular service or any particular subject for taxing. The celebrated judgment in Federation of Hotels and Restaurants case, cited supra, aptly covers the subject. In paragraph 20 the Apex Court observes that though the taxing laws are outside Article 14, however, having regard to the wide variety of diverse economic criteria that go into the formulation of a fiscal policy Legislature enjoys a wide latitude in the matter of selection of persons, subject-matter, events, etc. for taxation. The tests of the vice of discrimination in a taxing law are, accordingly, less rigorous. In examining the allegations of a hostile, discriminatory treatment what is looked into is not its phraseology, but the real effect of its provisions."

