( s)Any service provided or to be provided to any person, by a practising chartered accountant in his professional capacity, in any manner; Application of Saju’s Benefit Theory - The benefit of this service is in the form of service by a practising chartered accountant in his professional capacity, in any manner and, hence, there is no sale and, therefore, only service tax can be levied on the taxable value of this service. But, if the practising chartered accountant transfers any technology through a media like CD, paper, book or even through a person, it is transfer of property in goods for a consideration and, hence, it is a sale, taxable under the sales tax law. The technical know- how in a project report owned by him, expert opinion, etc., transferred in a media are goods produced out of human effort and the benefit received by the customer is in the form of property in such goods. These are technical know-how derived by the service provider and given in a container and, hence, they are goods. On the other hand, if the project report is prepared based on the data given by the customer, it is a service. The transfer makes the transferee the owner of such goods and the benefit is received by the transferee in the form of property in goods. The statement of accounts prepared by him, the audit report, certificates and investigation report, etc., in a media are report of services provided using some technology and the benefit is in the form of service and not in the form of goods as no technology is transferred to the customer. It is pertinent to note that the reports are the result of services like enquiry, verification or investigation, etc., and, hence, the benefit to the customer is the service. Similarly, the preparation of appeal and other petitions for the clients, representation for the clients and producing books of account and other data are services in which the client does not get benefit of transfer of technology, which the client can use elsewhere; as 